d activity  but these five are the main that have  been validated and are  found in many patients  this next slide just shows a little bit  of the consequences of these low  activity d2id polymorphisms  on the left hand side you can see a  figure showing the  the decrease in dpd metabolic activity  in an ex vivo screen in the top row  you'll see that  in 121 samples from non-carriers  their dpd activity range from ranged  from  a median of a little over 200.  below that you'll see the activity in 13  carriers of the star 2a  and in two carriers of the star 13 that  have much lower dpd activity  on the right hand side you'll see a  comparison of the  systemic five flower uracil exposure in  a single index patient who's a star to a  carrier  that's indicated by the diamonds  and that is in comparison in comparison  to six  control patients who are shown  in the triangle you can see the one  patient with the diamonds has much  higher  five a few concentrations and much  prolonged concentrations  relative to the control patients showing  that  carriers of this star 2a polymorphism or  other low activity polymorphisms  have increased systemic exposure to  5-fluorouracil  that increase in systemic exposure is  not now known to be affiliated with an  increase in severe toxicity  there have been many studies and i'll  show you some of those on the next slide  but this is  one of the largest and best studies this  is a retrospective  analysis conducted in a prospective  clinical trial called  ncctgn147  this trial had almost 3 000 patients  with colon cancer  who received standard combination  chemotherapy including 5-fluorouracil  these patients were genotyped for dpyd  star2a  and d949v two of the well-established  low activity polymorphisms  the primary outcome in this analysis was  severe  grade three plus five fluorouracil  related toxicity or adverse events  you can see on the bottom left the  results for the dpyd star 2a carriers  who had a near 90 risk of severe  toxicity  compared with about 30 percent in  non-carriers for an odds ratio or 15.  below that are the results for the  d-949v carriers  showing again about an 80 risk of severe  toxicity  versus about 30 in the non-carriers with  an odds ratio of  approximately nine you can see on the  right-hand side these results were  consistent across  all major subgroups and these results  are consistent with what has been seen  in several other cohorts  here's a study that just showed a  meta-analysis for  five excuse me four of the major  validated variants  so you can see in the upper left hand  corner the dpid star 2a  then going around clockwise the star 13  happy 3 and d9 for 9v  in each case these variants were  associated with a statistically  significant increase in severe toxicity  with adjusted  relative risks between an increase of  about sixty percent for happy three  to a four fold or higher increase for  star 13.  of course those have relatively wide  confidence intervals because of the  small numbers of patients each of these  analyses had at least 4 000 patients in  them  or highly statistically significant  in addition to the increase in severe  toxicity we  also know there's an increase in fatal  toxicity in carriers of dpyd variants  so i have here several reports in  major medical journals of case reports  of individual patients who passed away  of treatment or later found out to be  carriers of a dpyd polymorphism  these have been somewhat repeatedly  reported unfortunately  and there are some well-known stories  and websites that are dedicated to  patient stories  who were treated with standard dose of  fluoropyrimidines  and passed away or had severe toxicity  before finding out they were dpid  carriers  a recent analysis indicates that the  risk of fatal toxicity  in a d2id variant carrier is  approximately three percent  when given standard fluoroprimine dosing  in addition to all these retrospective  reports and retrospective analyses  there are now prospective clinical  studies that demonstrate the benefit  of pre-emptive or pre-treatment dpyd  testing  this is a prospective single-arm trial  of greater than 1 600 patients  who were pre-treatment tested for dpyd  star 2a  based on their star 2a genotype they  received either standard dose  fluoropyrimidine  if they were an impure 50  dose reduction if they carried one copy  or an 85  dose reduction if they carried two  copies of the star 2a variant  they were then dose escalated in future  cycles of treatment  as tolerated which is consistent with  the dose and guidelines  these patients who were pre-treatment  genotypes for  2a were compared with a historical  control  of dpyd star 2a carriers who were not  genotyped prior to treatment  and received its standard dosing this  comparison  is was made to demonstrate the benefits  of pre-treatment testing  there is also a comparison of the star  2a patients who received the just dosing  versus the wild type patients who  received standard dosing so we could see  some of the potential risks of dose  adjustment  and finally there is an analysis of  healthcare costs and i'll show you the  results of these  studies in the next slide  so in the primary analysis looking at  the star 2a carriers who received  adjusted dosing  compared with a historical control of  star 2a carriers  who received standard dosing there was a  significant reduction of grade 3 plus  toxicity  from about 73 in the historical control  to about 30 percent in the dose just  group  with a p-value of less than 0.001  that shows you the benefit of dose  adjustment in star 2a carriers  there is additionally a nominal  reduction in toxicity-related death  from a 10 estimate to 0 although this  did not reach statistical significance  due to the low number of events  again there are also some secondary  analyses to look at the risks of dose  adjustment  specifically the braid three plus  toxicity in our star2a carriers who  received adjust dosing  was compared with our grade three plus  toxicity in  our non-carriers who are wild-type  patients who received standard dosing  and you can see a normalization of  toxicity of about  25 in each group there is also a  normalization of drug concentrations  indicating that these patients the star  2a carriers who receive adjusted dosing  should have normalized efficacy as well  although that was an analysis done  in this publication  finally in their economic analysis  they estimated that dpyd screening prior  to treatment saves  about 60 dollars per patient  this data and other similar data from  other studies  has led to a publication by the clinical  pharmacogenetics implementation  consortium or cpec  describing their recommended dosing for  patients who carry dpyd  who have known dpid genotype so in the  top row here you see your normal  metabolizers that's two patients who  have  excuse me a patient who has two normal  alleles who has normal dpd activity  and should use the label recommended  dosing in administration  in the next row this is a simplified  version indicating that a patient  who carries one no function allele has  decreased tpd activity  and should receive a dose reduction of  about 50  followed by escalation as tolerated  finally in the bottom row our poor  metabolizers who have  no functional alleles have complete tpd  deficiency  they should either avoid use of  fluoropyrimidines or empirically reduce  the starting dose by about 90 percent  again followed by escalation as  tolerated and you can see the full cpic  dosing recommendations at the cpicker  pharm gkb website  so now i'll just quickly go over what  the general benefits and harms of  pre-treatment testing are  so everyone understands the benefits and  risks  of this strategy so again  starting with the benefits of dose  reduction in our  variant carriers which is about six  percent of patients  there is a reduction in grade three plus  toxicity from about  50 to 70 percent to about thirty percent  and a reduction in risk of death from  about three percent  to less than one percent we believe  there's an even greater decrease in the  homozygous patients  however precise estimates are currently  unavailable  of course the benefits of dose reduction  must be considered against the possible  harms or risk to  dose reduction the first potential harm  is the potential for decreased efficacy  particularly in the approximately 30  percent  of dpyd carriers who do not experience  severe toxicity  and were unnecessarily dose adjusted we  believe that there is a low concern for  diminished efficacy  for several reasons the first is there  are reports as i presented earlier  that the drug concentrations and  toxicity are normalized  in the dpyd variant carriers who receive  dose adjustment  in addition as i mentioned in the cpic  guidelines the  dosing guidelines recommend a dose  escalation to tolerability after cycle  one  so all patients should receive their  maximum tolerated dosing as  usual with chemotherapy  a second potential harm that is  sometimes sometimes discussed  is this so-called false sense of  security  there are about thirty percent of  patients who are non-carriers of dpyd  dpid variants who still experience  severe toxicity from standard  fluoropremium dosing  we believe that there is a low concern  for harm or  in fact no harm done to these patients  by genotyping  if you genotype these patients and there  is no genetic variant found  this does not change their dosing or  their monitoring in any way  whether or not this patient received  genotyping they still would receive  their standard fluorophamine dosing they  would still experience the severe  toxicity unfortunately  finally there's some concern for the  overall cost of genotyping  particularly because of the somewhat low  incidence of dpyd variant carrying  as i showed before there is some  evidence that pre-treatment cpid testing  actually saves money per patient in the  range about fifty dollars per patient  this is due to the high treatment costs  in dpyd carriers  who receive fluoropyrimidine dosing  which has been estimated to be  greater than two thousand dollars per  patient due to high risk of toxicity and  hospitalization  also note that there is a somewhat  recently approved antidote  for fluoroprimidine overdosing which is  called uridine trias tape  that treatment costs about eighty  thousand dollars per treatment so you  can see that even preventing  a single case of having to use that drug  makes this very financially viable  when we think about the evidence  supporting pre-treatment testing  we think about what is the evidence of  clinical utility  so clinical utility is defined as the  use of genetics to guide patient care  improves treatment outcomes  i believe that i have presented the  evidence showing that treatment outcomes  are improved in our dpyd variant  carriers  based on pre-treatment genotyping  there are two ways to demonstrate  clinical utility based on a report from  the institute of medicine  the first is a retrospective analysis of  a prospective clinical trial using  archived specimens  and i showed you that in this study from  the ncctg  the second way is a prospective clinical  trial in which the genetic test  directs patient management and i showed  you that in the prospective genotyping  study  the genotype dpyd star 2a and  demonstrated a reduction  in severe toxicity  therefore i think that we have met the  threshold of demonstrating clinical  utility  in both ways that are available and we  have clearly demonstrated  the clinical benefit of pre-treatment  dpid testing in patients who are going  to receive fluoropyrimidine treatment  many groups have agreed that we have  surpassed the threshold of clinical  utility and testing is now recommended  throughout europe  testing for dpyd or dpd activity is  required in france in the netherlands  and over the course of 2020 has been  recommended by  many of the medical agencies in europe  including the european medicines agency  however in the united states testing is  not recommended by any major body  including the fda asco or the nccn  and currently although we believe the  frequency is unknown  excuse me although we believe that  testing is uncommon the frequency of  testing in the united states is unknown  we did an internal survey at the  university of michigan of our medical  oncologists  to understand their practices and  beliefs about dpid or dpd testing  we asked this survey to 16 medical  oncologists who use fluoropyrimidine  who mostly came from breast or gi  oncology  here's a histogram of the number of  patients who  these medical oncologists started on a  new chemotherapy regimen  that contained afluropyrimidine so you  can see that many of our oncologists use  these  agents in about one patient per month  where some of our medical oncologists  use these agents in  10 or more patients per month  our first question was have our medical  oncologist  ever ordered a dpyd or dpd test prior to  starting fluoropremium chemotherapy  you can see that about 80 of our medical  oncologists had never ordered a test  prior to treatment  whereas about 20 percent had we then  asked of those  medical oncologists who have ordered  testing  how often they order testing and why two  of the oncologists two out of the three  who said they had ordered testing said  that they rarely do  one medical oncologist said that for  selected patients they order testing  based on performance status  we then ask several questions about  their beliefs about dpid genotype  and the pre-treatment testing the first  question  shown here in red on the right-hand side  is do you agree with the statement that  dpd deficiency increases toxicity risk  in patients through the fluoroprimitins  as you can see most of our clinicians  strongly agree with this statement  however the next question shown in the  purple was do you agree with the  statement that  testing of dpd or dpyd is useful to  inform treatment  and here you can see a much more normal  distribution  with most medical oncologists in the  middle saying they neither  neither agree nor disagree with that  statement only about a quarter  of medical oncologists agreeing with the  statement that pre-treatment testing is  useful  we then asked for about several factors  that we believed  might prevent medical oncologists from  ordering testing and i'll go through  some of the major highlights  so the factor that was endorsed to be  extremely or very important by most  medical oncologists was the lack of  clinical guidelines  from any of the major guideline  committees as i mentioned earlier  the next most common response was the  low prevalence of dpd deficiency  followed by the potential concern for  decreased efficacy from dose reduction  there were several other factors asked  about that had much lower concern  including testing cost  and a concern that dpd  testing will not reduce treatment  toxicity  one final question we asked about was  whether gpd deficiency is actionable  information  so here we asked if you knew that your  patient was dpd deficient  what would you do with that information  you can say that none of our medical  oncologists said that they would not do  anything  every medical oncologist said this would  be actionable information  with most saying they would decrease  fluoroprimine dosing  or some saying that they would consult a  clinical  guideline to find out what to do with  dosing or switch to a non-fluoroprimine  treatment  so you can see that when this  information is known it should  definitely be used because it's  actionable to guide treatment  so in conclusion our survey confirms  that  dpd deficiency is  known and understood to increase  toxicity risk  however few individuals or few medical  oncologists are  ordering dpd testing mostly because the  lack of clinical guidelines  this survey has been approved for  national distribution through swag and  we hope to have results in the near  future  now that we have uh demonstrated that  there is an  improvement in clinical care from  pre-treatment testing  i'll show you some of the work that  we're doing to increase the amount of  pre-treatment testing that's being done  the current status is that few  fluoropromine treated patients  are tested prior to treatment there are  some sites and clinicians who i'm aware  of  that are ordering testing and there's  some advocate efforts to increase  the patient awareness to ask for testing  one of the things that we've done at the  university of michigan local cancer  center  is to use our existing dpyd data to make  sure that patients who have  known dpd deficiency are being treated  accordingly  so we have a large program called the  michigan genomics initiative that has  it's a genetic data repository from  greater than 60 000 patients  who have genome-wide genotyping  information this information cannot be  used for clinical decision making  however it can be used for  clinical research what we have done is  we've set up a clinical research study  that is essentially an implementation  project  where we have found we have received the  names  of the approximately three thousand  patients who are known carriers of one  of the five dpyd variants  based on this mgi data we've set up  automated alerts in our medical record  so anytime one of these dpyd carriers is  diagnosed  with a fluoropyrimidine treated tumor  our study team gets notified  we then contact the medical oncologist  to confirm this patient is going to  receive a floral primitive  and enroll the patient on our study and  order and pay for their confirmatory  dpid testing through the mayo clinic  upon confirming their dpid status we  return this information to the medical  oncologist  within the electronic medical record and  provide them a dosing recommendation  so that this patient can receive safe  and effective dosing  we launched this study in september of  2020 and  launched our automated alert system  we immediately found that there was a  dpyd carrier  in the mgi data who had previously  that week been newly diagnosed with  colon cancer  we immediately contacted medical  oncologist and confirmed that this  patient is expected to receive  combination chemotherapy including a  five-floor uracil  we then enrolled the subject the  following day and sent their dpid test  and returned that information with the  confirmed dpyd happy3 carrier status to  medical oncologist  within seven days of launching our alert  demonstrating  the efficiency and rapidity of getting  this information for patients  in terms of the efficiency it cost us  six hundred dollars to  identify and confirm one dpyd variant  carrier  compared with about twelve thousand  dollars per dpyd variant carrier  uh identified in doing pre-treatment  screening of all patients  we plan to expand this process to  ugtp1a1 and other genes  that are actionable in patients with  cancer  a much larger pool of patients could be  could benefit if we added dpid  to somatic and germline cancer genetic  testing panels  this is a concept that i published on  last year in the journal of the national  comprehensive cancer network  so in terms of germline cancer panels we  know that many cancer patients undergo  multi-gene germline testing to find  cancer predisposition  according to the nccn guidelines all  patients with pancreatic cancer and many  patients with  colorectal breast and ovarian cancer  should receive some kind of germline  testing  these are all tumors that are commonly  treated with the fluoroprimine treatment  the nccn guidelines recommend multi-gene  testing  and specify that not all genes included  on the panels are necessarily actionable  i would argue that dpy are actionable  and should be included on these panels  there are some germline panels that do  include dpid  including the panel from color genomics  however others do not  including panels for germline genetic  testing by  ambry and nvite we are very interested  in working with these companies  to add dpyd to their existing panels  similarly there are many patients with  cancer that undergo somatic  or tumor genetic testing to select a  targeted treatment  we believe that by adding dpyd to these  cancer genetic panels we could  achieve the benefits of dpid testing for  limited additional cost  there are some tumor genetic panels  including that from tempus  which also include germline testing and  have included dpyd  however however other commonly used  panels such as that from foundation 1  are only tumor testing and do not  include dpyd  we are currently working with a  foundation group to try to  work with some of these companies to try  to convince them to add dpyd  so patients can benefit from this  information  finally we really hope to get to the  point where all fluoroprominene treated  patients  are genotyped for gpyd prior to  treatment  in order to do so again based on what we  heard from medical oncologists  we need to get the guidelines panels on  board with recommending fluoropyrimidine  uh excuse me dpyd testing in patients  who are going to receive a  fluoropyrimidine  specifically we are interested in  getting recommendations from the nccn  and or the fda  the nccn treatment guidelines for colon  cancer  currently include the following  the following quotes so the colon cancer  guidelines state that individuals with  variants of dpid have a significantly  elevated risk for severe  life-threatening toxicity  acknowledging the clinical validity or  the increase in risk in these carriers  they go on to say that pre-treatment  dpid testing showed a significant  reduction  in the risk of grade 3 toxicity  acknowledging the clinical utility of  pre-treatment testing  they then concluded by saying that  universal pre-treatment testing  remains controversial and the mccm panel  does not support it at this time  despite acknowledging the clinical  validity in clinical utility  we submitted a request in 2019 outlining  some of the more recent data  recommending that germline dpid testing  be added to the routine pre-treatment  workup for all patients  likely to receive a flow of pyrimidine  followed by dosing according to the cpic  as i uh presented earlier  their response was the panel did not  support the inclusion of dpyd testing as  a routine component of  the pre-treatment workup and the final  vote was unanimous of 0-22  they did not give us any reasoning for  this  however what we understand is that the  nccm guidelines are based on current  clinical practice  so here we have a classic chicken  situation  so the clinician practice is not to  order testing  and they say this is because of a lack  of clinical guidelines from the mccn  then ccn does not update their  guidelines to include  testing because their guidelines reflect  what happens in clinical practice  and here neither group is ready to make  this first step because they're waiting  for the other  to lead the way  we hope that one day to break this log  jam excuse me one way to break this log  jam  is to get a recommendation from the fda  for pre-treatment dpyd screening  so the fda package inserts for 530  uracil and cape cytobean  were updated in 2016 to acknowledge that  patients  with certain mutations in dpid are at  increased risk for  severe early onset toxicity that can be  fatal and that patients with partial dpd  activity may also have increased risk  which i showed you from  earlier studies in the patient  counseling  information of the package insert it  advises that patients should notify  their healthcare provider  if they have known dpd deficiency the  issue is that patients would not know  this  prior to receiving the flurry of  pyrimidine we have submitted  a fda citizen petition with mr ken  cerpenette a well-known patient advocate  for  d2id testing and several other leaders  in this field  we recommended adding a black black box  warning  for the toxicity risk and to recommend  pre-treatment dpd screening  in patients in patients who are going to  receive fluoroprimitin  we also requested that they recommend  dpd-guided dosing  from cpic or other such dosing  guidelines  there's a qr code that you can scan with  your phone  so you can read our petition and there  is currently an open comment period so  you can add supportive  or not supportive comments to the fda  changing the package insert to recommend  pre-treatment testing  thank you much for thank you very much  for attending  my presentation here's that qr code if  you'd like to comment on our fda  petition  we really would appreciate your support  and i look forward to  answering any questions from the  audience thank you very much

